format 1
adapter statements 1
unit: radioactivity

statement radioactivity-s01
verdict: myth
statement: Radioactivity is something people invented in the last hundred years.

explanation:
    Radioactivity was here long before people. It is a natural process.

statement radioactivity-s02
verdict: fact
statement: Rocks, air, food and even your own body contain a little natural radioactivity.

explanation:
    Natural radioactivity is all around us, and inside us.

statement radioactivity-s03
verdict: myth
statement: Radioactive always means dangerous.

explanation:
    It depends on the kind and the amount. Radioactive does not automatically mean dangerous.

statement radioactivity-s04
verdict: fact
statement: An unstable nucleus changes all by itself, and nobody can say exactly when one nucleus will change.

explanation:
    The change is natural and unpredictable for a single nucleus.

statement radioactivity-s05
verdict: myth
statement: An unstable nucleus has to be hit by something before it can decay.

explanation:
    Natural decay needs no push. The nucleus changes on its own.

statement radioactivity-s06
verdict: fact
statement: Alpha, beta and gamma are three different kinds of radiation.

explanation:
    They come out of a changing nucleus, but they are three different things.

statement radioactivity-s07
verdict: myth
statement: Alpha, beta and gamma are just three names for the same thing.

explanation:
    They are different from each other. That is why they have different names.

statement radioactivity-s08
verdict: fact
statement: Half-life is about a crowd of nuclei, not a timer inside each atom.

explanation:
    In a big group, half of the unstable nuclei change in one half-life. You cannot say which ones.

statement radioactivity-s09
verdict: myth
statement: After one half-life, all the radioactive nuclei in a sample are gone.

explanation:
    After one half-life, half of them are left, not none.

statement radioactivity-s10
verdict: fact
statement: In a decay chain, every change makes a different nucleus, and nothing simply vanishes.

explanation:
    One decay is not always the end. The new nucleus can change again.
